      <dl class="phases">
        <dt>Now to mid-2027</dt>
        <dd>A Canadian consortium qualifies through Shared Services Canada’s intake cycles. Definitions line up across departments. Partners sign on.</dd>
        <dt>2027 to 2029</dt>
        <dd>A certified surface tier opens on a multi-year government contract, with Indigenous equity at the table. A feasibility study looks at one site in the Shield.</dd>
        <dt>2029 to 2035</dt>
        <dd>If the study holds up, the continuity tier comes online under the Shield. Sovereign AI capacity grows with demand.</dd>
      </dl>
      <p>France’s experience is a useful guide. S3NS, a French sovereign cloud built with a large foreign technology partner, earned France’s national certification in December 2025, three years after it was founded.<sup><a href="#note-28">28</a></sup> Starting early is most of the battle, and Canada has already started.</p>

      <h2><span class="eyebrow">What we can do together</span> Canada doesn’t need to be angry to be sovereign. It needs to finish what it started.</h2>
      <p>The public servants who wrote that sentence in 2018 were right. The people building the answer now are doing hard work, mostly out of sight. The rest of us can help by understanding it, backing it, and showing up when there’s something to build.</p>
      <p>Somewhere in the Shield there is a place that could hold the house key for the whole country. I’d like to help find it.</p>
      <p><strong>If you’re working on any of this, I’d like to hear from you. If I got something wrong, I want to hear that even more.</strong></p>
      <figure class="mark">
        <BearDual />
      </figure>
      <p class="sign">Jesse James</p>
      <p>Northern Temper</p>

      <h2 id="words"><span class="eyebrow">Words used here</span> Eight terms, plainly.</h2>
      <dl>
        <dt>Protected A and Protected B</dt>
        <dd>The government’s labels for sensitive information that is not classified. A is information whose release could hurt a person or a business; B is information whose release could cause serious harm. Most everyday government work sits here.</dd>
        <dt>Secret and Top Secret</dt>
        <dd>Classified information, whose release could harm the country. It lives on separate government networks, not the public internet.</dd>
        <dt>Cloud service provider</dt>
        <dd>A company that rents out computing and storage from its own data centres. CSP for short.</dd>
        <dt>CLOUD Act</dt>
        <dd>A 2018 United States law that lets US authorities require US-based companies to hand over data they control, wherever in the world it is stored. Countries can sign agreements with the US that set rules for these requests; Canada has been negotiating one since 2022.<sup><a href="#note-2">2</a></sup></dd>
        <dt>Invitation to Qualify</dt>
        <dd>A first step in federal buying: firms apply to be recognized as able to supply something, before any contract is offered.<sup><a href="#note-9">9</a></sup></dd>
        <dt>Off-take, or purchase commitment</dt>
        <dd>A promise to buy a set amount of something for a set time. Lenders will finance a building against one.</dd>
        <dt>Air-gapped</dt>
        <dd>Physically disconnected from any outside network. Nothing gets in or out except by hand.</dd>
        <dt>Power usage effectiveness</dt>
        <dd>All the power a data centre draws, divided by the power its computers use. 1.0 would mean none is spent on cooling or anything else.</dd>
      </dl>

